      "dom_id": "question-43343",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266178/assets/192cd7673f9186480bd6ab9955e56eab78b8a4e3bbec9d8d824922b5fd9c0527.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{15\\sqrt{3}}{2}{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ Determine $m∠A.$",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=5\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC=6\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $m∠A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{15\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 6\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{15\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =15\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{15}\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\cancel{15}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡A & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ m∠A & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
