    {
      "dom_id": "question-72756",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266178/assets/721f8231c38ba5937b4e2f80233f83ecb52605388897291d14968064f214a4c4.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest tenth, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$",
      "worked_solution": "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266178/assets/2bdf5043e8bd25884eeda042c276fad2e5fa267e1f9f19d9271564576965d09a.png)\n\n \n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 10\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{110}^{∘} \\\\  & =30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{110}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈28.190\\,7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 13\\cdot 15\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{58}^{∘} \\\\  & =97.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{58}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈82.684\\,7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & =28.190\\,7+82.684\\,7 \\\\  & =110.875\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest tenth, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $110.9.$",
