    {
      "dom_id": "question-72764",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266178/assets/f53996727138b9442ac041cf492618db8287b54ba652c4f9e375cdde27dc1862.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $34{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$   What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 15\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{52}^{∘} \\\\  & =67.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{52}^{∘} \\\\  & =53.190… \\\\  & ≈53{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =53-34 \\\\  & =19{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
